AB  - Large Language Models are widely integrated into chatbots, enterprise applications, and automation systems. However, these systems are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks - malicious instructions embedded in text, files, images, or translated content, attempting to override system rules. This paper presents the design and implementation of the AI Prompt Injection Attack Tester: a multi-modal system for detecting and mitigating Prompt Injection attempts against Large Language Models. While the ubiquitous deployment of LLMs has introduced a critical security vulnerability, where untrusted user input hijacks the model’s policy and system prompt, we introduce the following concept: a Heuristic-Based Prompt Injection Detection Engine integrated into a pre-processing guardrail layer. The PIDE applies a specialized ruleset, which includes instruction-overriding pattern matching and sensitive keyword identification, to incoming data for a risk score. Importantly, the system normalizes and scans inputs from four different modalities: direct text, embedded code comments/literals, image text via OCR, and text that has been processed through a mock translation service. On detection, a Mitigation Pipeline applies sequential steps of sanitization, instruction stripping, and policy-based blocking prior to any input reaching the LLM. As confirmed within a safe, mock environment for LLMs, experimental results validate that this approach provides both successful detection and neutralization of malicious instructions across all types of input tested in this work. This paper confirms that a robust, multi-modal pre-processing policy layer is required as an integral component of a broad defense-in-depth strategy against sophisticated Prompt Injection attacks.
ER  - 

