AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into enterprise applications but remain vulnerable to direct and indirect prompt injection attacks. Existing defense mechanisms often rely on static filtering and lack continuous verification. This paper proposes AdaptiveTrustGuard, a Zero Trust framework that integrates prompt validation, semantic similarity detection, retrieval verification, context isolation, adaptive trust scoring, temporal attack analysis, and policy enforcement, continuously verifying each interaction before it reaches the LLM. Initial evaluation on a manually labelled set achieved 60% recall, but testing on the independent deepset/prompt-injections benchmark exposed poor generalization (8.3% recall). Replacing fixed thresholds with a classifier trained on prompt embeddings and calibrated via a validation split recovered strong performance (78.3% recall, 92.2% precision, ROC-AUC 0.943 on deepset), exceeding an external baseline detector, while achieving 61.7% recall on the larger, more realistic PromptShield benchmark. These findings demonstrate the applicability of Zero Trust principles to LLM security while highlighting the necessity of multi-benchmark validation
ER  - 

