AB  - Large language model-powered mental health chatbots can improve mental health assistance, but they pose ethical hazards such crisis management, misleading empathy, and harmful discrimination. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures and guardrail big language models are used to optimise mental health counselling prompts while ensuring ethical alignment. Domain specialists in crisis management, coping methods, psychoeducation, and empathetic reactions are combined with reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) under ethical limitations. Moderated input, expert routing, guardrail validation, and output screening are performed via a multi-layer safety pipeline. A publicly available mental health counselling dataset shows 94.3% safety compliance, 87.2% contextual adaptability, and 91.5% crisis detection sensitivity with 42% lower computing cost than dense baselines. Ethics-by-design improves safety without compromising therapeutic quality by bridging the gap between ethical AI concepts and susceptible domain implementation.
ER  - 

