AB  - Large language models (LLMs) deployed as autonomous agents with tool access, retrieval pipelines, and persistent memory expose an expanded attack surface. We formalize prompt injection as a control-boundary violation arising from the conflation of trusted instructions and untrusted data within LLM context windows. We introduce a threat model spanning user input, retrieval pipelines, tool outputs, and persistent memory, and develop a structured taxonomy of attack vectors. We conduct a controlled empirical evaluation across three system architectures (chatbot, retrieval-augmented system, autonomous agent) and three major LLM families (GPT-4, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Gemini 1.5 Pro), totaling 2,100 trials ($N=50$ per configuration). Results show a monotonic increase in attack success as system autonomy expands, with memory poisoning reaching 94% (95% CI: 83.8-98.2%) in autonomous configurations. Through systematic ablation of six mitigation strategies, we find that prompt-level defenses provide limited protection $(\leq 3.2$ percentage points), while architectural controls-specifically tool permission gating, memory write filtering, and provenance tracking-reduce memory poisoning from 94% to 11%. These findings provide strong empirical evidence that prompt injection is best understood as a system-level vulnerability requiring external policy enforcement rather than prompt engineering alone.
ER  - 

