AB  - Prompt injection attacks is an important security problem in healthcare-specific large language model systems(LLMs). The injected prompt can be used to control the behavior of the model or to leak sensitive data. This vulnerability increases with centralized training pipelines that collect patient prompts on a single server, which leads to data leakage and model poisoning. In this work, we examine Federated Learning (FL) as a privacy-preserving and decentralized prompt injection detection defense for medical chatbots. Using the SPML Healthcare Chatbot Prompt Injection Dataset, we fine-tune a RoBERTa-base classifier in conditions of centralized, IID (Independent and Identically Distributed), federated and Non-IID (Non-Independent and Non-Identically Distributed) federated learning. The experimental results show that the federated model has comparable performance with centralized training, with accuracy of 0.957 and F1-score of 0.964 under IID conditions. The realistic case scenario of Non-IID client distribution was evaluated across 3 different client configurations, the model is able to maintain stable performance with an average performance accuracy of 0.956, precision of 0.942, recall of 0.987, F1-Score of 0.964 and AUC of 0.994. Moreover, the federated setup shows consistent convergence between the communication rounds despite the data heterogeneity. These results suggest that transformer-based federated learning provides an effective and privacy-preserving solution for detecting prompt injection attacks in distributed healthcare conversational AI systems. This work contributes to Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 3: Good Health and Wellbeing, SDG 9: Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure, and SDG 16: Peace, Justice and Strong Institutions by enhancing the security and privacy of AI-driven healthcare technologies.
ER  - 

