AB  - The popularity of large language model (LLM)-based chatbots has increased their vulnerability to adversarial prompt attacks that programmable affect the model's behaviour and produce scripted responses. The paper introduces an Explainable Transformer-Based Detection (ETD) framework that is meant to detect and explain adversarial prompts on the fly. The proposed system offers a BERT-based contextual encoder and an attention-based explanation layer, which captures fine-grained semantical dependencies and assigns model decisions on attribute models to certain token influences. The experiment was done on a curated set of 12,000 benign and 8,000 adversarial prompts produced using paraphrasing, prompt injection, and contextual perturbation methods. The ETD framework attained an accuracy of 96.8, a precision of 95.4, a recall of 97.2, and an F1-score of 96.3, which are 5.7 and 8.1 points higher than the traditional LSTM- and CNN-based baselines, respectively. In addition, the built-in explainability component offers the visual interpretations at the token level, which makes it easier to be transparent in detecting adversarial intent. The findings prove that the given ETD model is rather effective in terms of balancing the performance of the detection and the interpretability of the system, which makes contemporary chatbot systems more robust and trustworthy.
