AB  - College students experience high levels of academic, financial, and social stress. Yet many of them face barriers to accessing campus counseling services, including limited availability, scheduling conflicts, and stigma. This work investigates whether AI-based conversational tools can serve as non-clinical, everyday stress-management support. This study surveyed 120 undergraduate and graduate students at a public urban university to assess perceived usefulness, trust, and ethical concerns regarding AI stress-support tools. Findings indicate that 69.9% of students are willing to use such a tool, with trust rising to 73% when the university endorses it. However, comfort around discussing sensitive topics such as trauma or support during crisis dropped significantly- 35% and 22% respectively. Informed by these findings, this study proposes a prototype of LLM-powered chatbot integrated with a heightened layer of safety guardrail architecture, including input filtering, classification, output review, and crisis escalation, demonstrating the implications of responsible AI deployment in higher-education well-being.
