AB  - Existing Large Language Model (LLM) based systems for mental health support are often unsafe and unreliable, primarily due to the absence of dynamic, user-controlled memory. This limitation leads to inconsistent therapeutic continuity and undermines the user trust necessary for forming meaningful therapeutic alliances. This paper introduces an Agentic Therapeutic Agent designed to address these challenges through a modular architecture that prioritizes safety, transparency, and long-term relationship building. The proposed framework's core feature is a secure Long-Term Memory system with a user-facing control panel and pathways for human therapist oversight. Initial development has produced two foundational, non-integrated modules: a rudimentary conversational AI and an effective crisis detection model intended to function as the first component of the agent's Safety & Guardrail Layer. The central contribution of this project is the design, implementation, and validation of this transparent memory architecture, which will integrate the conversational and safety modules. This approach directly addresses the critical gaps of memory and trust in therapeutic AI, demonstrating a tangible pathway toward a more ethically guided and effective support tool.
ER  - 

